# Line endings. Git's Windows default (core.autocrlf=true) rewrites line endings
# on checkout. For most repositories that is cosmetic. For this one it is not:
# reference/rederivable-manifest/ is a digest-pinned artifact, and rewriting its
# bytes changes what SHA-256 is computed over.
#
# Demonstrated on a real cold clone, not assumed. CRLF-ifying that directory makes
# all three pinned manifests fail with "e_digest mismatch: archived evidence
# differs from frozen evidence" and moves the Merkle anchor root from
# 4e49f63a17cf... to 576019221d5d.... The failure is indistinguishable from
# tampering -- the worst possible way for an encoding bug to present itself in a
# forensics tool, and a trap laid for every Windows contributor to a public repo.
#
# Scope check, so the comment does not overclaim: the policy content-hash on the
# audit row is computed over a normalised snapshot read back from the database
# (policy_loader._normalized_snapshot), not over config/*.yaml bytes, so it was
# never exposed to this. The vendored artifact is the live exposure. The repo-wide
# rule below is contributor hygiene and defence in depth as the crypto epic
# (ND-001 -> ND-015 -> ND-017) lands more digest surface.

# Repository-wide: LF in the repository AND in every working tree, on every
# platform. Not merely "normalise on commit" -- eol=lf also fixes the checkout
# side, which is where the corruption actually happens.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The vendored, digest-pinned artifact from core (pinned at v3). These bytes are
# not ours to normalise: they are verbatim as core shipped them, and validate.py
# recomputes SHA-256 over them. `-text` disables conversion in BOTH directions,
# so no filter -- present or future, ours or a contributor's -- can touch them.
#
# Deliberately `-text` rather than `text eol=lf`: the vendoring guarantee is
# "the bytes core shipped, unaltered", and a future artifact revision may carry
# a fixture that legitimately contains CR (the canonicalisation rules cover
# string handling). Normalising would eat it. This directory is replaced
# wholesale at each revision, never hand-edited, so self-healing normalisation
# buys nothing here and could corrupt a deliberate byte.
#
# Do not "tidy" this into `text eol=lf`. Named by core in Response 007: this is
# E10's two-discipline rule operating at the version-control layer. The vendored
# artifact is RECEIVED data -- core's bytes, frozen verbatim, never normalised --
# exactly as params_json/payload_json are frozen verbatim at ingress. Our own
# source above is GENERATED data, and generated data normalises. Same rule, two
# layers.
reference/rederivable-manifest/** -text

# Exported patches are byte-exact diffs; CRLF corruption makes `git apply` fail
# or, worse, apply a subtly different hunk.
patches/*.patch -text

# Core's memo archive. Received data, same as the vendored artifact -- and from
# Response 008 these files carry `Integrity: sha256(body) = <hex>` over their own
# bytes, so version control joined their trust path the moment the footer shipped.
# Prose was exempt only while nothing depended on its bytes; that ended retroactively.
#
# Found by the forensics session and relayed (Core to Forensics 009 section 3): this
# directory was falling under `* text=auto eol=lf` while reference/ and patches/ were
# already fenced. Everything here is LF today so nothing was corrupted, but the first
# CRLF memo would fail its own digest and present as CORE SENDING A BAD DIGEST -- the
# same tampering-shaped diagnosis as the artifact, aimed at the wrong party.
docs/from_core/** -text

# The relay's byte-preserving drop point. Disk-copying the file is what finally
# delivered Core->Forensics 010 intact after two renderings of it arrived corrupted,
# so this path is now part of the received-data trust path and is fenced before it is
# used again rather than after.
docs/incoming/** -text
